    "chunk_size_bytes": 1837,
    "chunk_text": "\u2018Where the Kingfisher Waits\u2019 \u2014 A Collection of Poems\n\nWhere the Kingfisher Waits\u2019 by Nirma de Silva Wijeyeratne is a recently published book which is a collection of poems about journeys inspired by nature, history and place. With evocative description, lived experience and a deep appreciation of natural and cultural heritage, the poet takes the reader from rice fields of golden plumes, to white stupas, to tables overflowing with jasmine and lotus.\n\nOne of the acclaimed international authors to comment on the book is Helen Macdonald, the author of \u2018H is for Hawk\u2019 which won the Costa Book of the Year award. She has been to Sri Lanka for both the Galle Literary Festival and the Ceylon Literary Festival, experienced Sri Lanka\u2019s nature first-hand and met the poet in Sri Lanka. Commenting on the collection, she says \u2018Poems woven from the bonds that draw together memory and place, history and nature in and around our human hearts. De Silva Wijeyeratne possesses an imagist\u2019s eye, a deep awareness of the beauty and complexity of the natural world, and an attentive, careful poetic practice that uncovers a great richness of meaning from what lies all around us.\u2019\n\nThe poems published in a 65 page soft cover book celebrate nature in vivid imagery. \u2018From an island in the lake, you rise all at once, to form aerial patterns.\u2019 Local words bring authenticity to the setting; \u2018by a waterhole under shady kumbuk trees I quietly watch \u2014 kingfishers cloaked in skill and turquoise.\u2019 John Gimlette, the author of \u2018Elephant Complex\u2019 is another international author who comments on the collection saying \u2018Exquisite poems from the heart and the forest. Nirma de Silva Wijeyeratne delicately evokes both the beauty and the sadness of life in these precious lines. I have so enjoyed reading these poems.\u2019"
  },
