    "chunk_size_bytes": 1495,
    "chunk_text": "Pioneer of Sri Lankan Abstract Art\n\nIt is wilth great joy that I delve into my memories of the inimitable art teacher H A Karunatne. He was of course a teacher in the country\u2019s formal education system and yet I am compelled to describe him as one who went beyond his professional calling to guide countless students, especially given my personal interactions with him. My compulsion is also driven by what I\u2019ve heard what his formal students have said about him. He stood beyond the perimeters of the countless formal and traditional teachers we encountered. He stood apart, in this and other ways.\n\nI consider it an honour to have known H A Karunaratne, a man with an advanced, balanced, calm, strong and brave intellect. The first meeting was quite by chance. Higher education was an unrealized dream for me due to a technical glitch that was necessary for institutional continuity, to put it mildly. I nevertheless got an opportunity to attend a non-traditional university, the Divaina of Upali Newspapers and its Sunday edition, Divaina Irida Sangrahaya. This was in the year 1981.\n\nI was hired as the editorial artist but I always wanted to explore regions that were outside the ambit of an artist\u2019s role in print media. I expressed this desire and following discussions on the matter, the Editor-in-Chief Edmund Ranasinghe, Deputy Editor Dayasena Gunasinghe, and the then Managing Director Leslie Dharmaratne agreed to support me and as importantly accorded me the necessary freedom."
  },
