    "md5": "ddca73e4f29f4c9eef3b6340cb2f163e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1719,
    "chunk_text": "He was composed. The discipline developed through exercise served as a filter for his energy and strength which, thereafter, he could pour into the creative exercise. This seems to be what many in Sri Lanka cannot understand. It is the congealed energy filtered from a unique and unforgiving regimen that we find in his creative work.\n\nH A Karunaratne, having studied under the great teachers at the Government College of Fine Arts obtained a scholarship to further study his craft overseas. Later he served as a senior lecturer at the Aesthetic University. More important than any of this is his life as an artist. From what I know, he was quite industrious. And he embraced a wide spectrum of forms. He studied and while studying ventured into graphic art, print, sculpture and pure art even as he was the pioneer of aforementioned abstract and contemporary art.\n\nOnce I helped him set up a large painting on a wall of the Rupavahini Corporation lobby. He found a broom from somewhere and started sweeping the tiled floor. It was done. He stepped back a bit and gazed upon the painting for a moment. Then he came towards me.\n\n\u2018Shantha, someone might come after we\u2019ve gone and thinking it is incomplete may proceed to \u201cfinish it\u201d don\u2019t you think?\u2019\n\nFor art to be appreciated people should be able to associate with it closely, so to speak. There are certain preconditions for this to happen. There should be good art galleries. Good art should be exhibited. There should be discussions and debates about art and new trends in art. There should be good art critics. These are not conditions that artists can create. H A Karunaratne did what he could to the best of his ability and strength. He did his duty."
  },
