    "time_ut": 1778348400.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-05-09-islandlk-9b229a0f-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0713ee7a6c8c7c331f542d64974ac2df",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1933,
    "chunk_text": "On the political front, the government came to power promising a \u2018system change\u2019 and ambitious ideas for modernization. However, what we are witnessing is not a system change, but rather system maintenance. This so-called \u2018system maintenance\u2019 lacks the human capacity required to deliver meaningful results. At present, the entire political discourse appears to revolve around a single buzzword: AKD (Anura Kumara Dissanayake). In my view, this alone is insufficient to rescue an economy from bankruptcy or steer the country through the turbulent period we are facing.\n\nMaintaining the status quo is not enough; we require growth of around 10% to 11% to double our capacity and meet debt obligations. Talking about 5% growth is inadequate when the goal is to transform the economy.\n\nIn addition, there are external shocks such as the situation in the Middle East. Every government faces external challenges; this is not unique to the present administration. What matters is the capacity, resources, and knowledge to manage such shocks when they arise.\n\nQ: How would you describe the financial discipline of the current NPP administration?\n\nA: It is interesting. Ranil Wickremesinghe established a very high benchmark for government revenue through heavy taxation, perhaps excessively so. Now, Anura Kumara Dissanayake is essentially maintaining that same framework from the 2023\u20132025 period. While I appreciate their adherence to fiscal discipline, revenue collection alone is not sufficient.\n\nUnder the Public Fiscal Responsibility Act, expenditure is capped at 13% of GDP. If GDP growth stagnates and currency depreciation occurs, we effectively spend less than in the previous year. What we need is increased spending to stimulate growth, as expenditure drives economic expansion. That element is currently missing.\n\nQ: You have been vocal about the Central Bank. What is your main criticism of its financial management?"
  },
