    "md5": "9ea606fa3a0c00542f26d684f08df35a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1810,
    "chunk_text": "Under the Public Fiscal Responsibility Act, expenditure is capped at 13% of GDP. If GDP growth stagnates and currency depreciation occurs, we effectively spend less than in the previous year. What we need is increased spending to stimulate growth, as expenditure drives economic expansion. That element is currently missing.\n\nQ: You have been vocal about the Central Bank. What is your main criticism of its financial management?\n\nRecently, when officials appeared before the Committee on Public Finance, their professionalism was severely lacking. They were unable to adequately explain how a bank had misled them for a period of three years.\n\nQ: What specific reforms are you proposing for the Central Bank?\n\nA: I have proposed the establishment of a Parliamentary Select Committee to ensure that the Central Bank\u2019s mandate shifts from a largely \u201claissez-faire\u201d approach to one focused on growth, employment generation, and reserve accumulation. The Bank should operate in close coordination with the Ministry of Finance rather than in isolation.\n\nWe cannot allow unelected officials to dictate monetary policy while expecting elected representatives to remain silent. A country cannot be developed solely through anti-inflationary, contractionary policies. What we need is a supply-side, pro-growth strategy similar to what we observe in India under Prime Minister Modi.\n\nQ: Speaking of India, how do you interpret their recent election results in relation to Sri Lanka\u2019s future?\n\nA: Voters across the world are increasingly cynical. In Sri Lanka, the public is in a state of uncertainty; they do not fully understand the complexities of governance, which has become far more challenging than many realize. This is a period in which proven competence must take precedence over optimistic narratives."
  },
