    "md5": "7fcf4d1de9ca2934c99ef606efd80147",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1753,
    "chunk_text": "A: Voters across the world are increasingly cynical. In Sri Lanka, the public is in a state of uncertainty; they do not fully understand the complexities of governance, which has become far more challenging than many realize. This is a period in which proven competence must take precedence over optimistic narratives.\n\nQ: What is your assessment of the current situation in the power and energy sector, given rising prices and issues with procurement and substandard coal?\n\nA: Governance challenges are not unique to any one administration; every government encounters new problems over time. However, the current crisis stems from policy misdirection. Previously, renewable energy supported by Battery Energy Storage Systems (BESS) was considered the most viable way to reduce foreign exchange pressure and utilize domestic resources.\n\nUnfortunately, the government adopted an anti-renewable energy stance, resulting in dependence on substandard coal and costly thermal power generation. This has led to an estimated financial impact of 110 to 120 billion rupees. This burden must either be absorbed by the government which lacks sufficient fiscal space, given that it amounts to roughly 0.5% of GDP or passed on to consumers.\n\nQ: Regarding the political landscape, what are your views on discussions about the UNP and SJB collaborating or merging into a single party?\n\nA: Every major political party in this country has had the opportunity to govern and demonstrate its capabilities. My \u2018revolutionary approach\u2019 is that all parties should come together around a set of minimum national objectives. While this may appear impractical, the reality is that the maximum number of parties must unite to address the declining expectations of the public."
  },
