    "md5": "1d43708688a11f9eb2a48d0232d7e179",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1737,
    "chunk_text": "A: Every major political party in this country has had the opportunity to govern and demonstrate its capabilities. My \u2018revolutionary approach\u2019 is that all parties should come together around a set of minimum national objectives. While this may appear impractical, the reality is that the maximum number of parties must unite to address the declining expectations of the public.\n\nQ: Have you shared this vision with senior political leaders, and how have they responded?\n\nA: The reality is evident; those who fail to recognize these changes will eventually become irrelevant. I communicate these ideas in a professional manner, rather than acting like a \u2018bull in a china shop.\u2019 The central message is that the concerns of the people cannot be ignored; they must be acknowledged, addressed, and resolved.\n\nA: I urge people to believe in what is achievable rather than what is unrealistic. This country has a long history of promises\u2014rice at 25 cents, eight kilos of grains, cheap sugar, and bread at Rs 3.50\u2014that ultimately erode economic foundations. We must learn from development models in countries such as Indonesia, Thailand, Malaysia, Singapore, and Vietnam, which have moved away from welfare-heavy approaches.\n\nWe must shift from distributing poverty to creating and sharing prosperity. While other nations have focused on growth, we have too often moved in the opposite direction. That is the fundamental change required. I also call upon the younger generation, those under 30, to adopt a more revolutionary and disciplined mindset. They must not allow impulsive thinking to guide their decisions. Instead, they should evaluate performance, rely on knowledge, and observe global trends to broaden their perspectives."
