    "md5": "c4d770773c34b0e8976ca69283a955cd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2016,
    "chunk_text": "Three days later, the Chairman called me. The new CEO had walked into his house with his resignation letter. The job had overwhelmed him. I said nothing. There was no need to say, \u201cI told you so.\u201d\n\nBy now, with the resignation of the Chairman, I was once again made the non-executive Chairman.\n\nEventually, we operated with acting CEOs until the Japanese directors persuaded me to take over full-time as Chairman and CEO. I initially resisted, as I was still engaged in restructuring the Merchant Bank of Sri Lanka, which was my full-time job. I had given my word to the staff that I would not leave until their suspended benefits were restored. Once that promise was fulfilled and dividends were paid to shareholders after many years, I moved to Dankotuwa full-time.\n\nLooking back, this episode reinforced a simple but critical truth: selecting the right leader is not about qualifications alone. It is about fit\u2014fit with the organisation, its people, its culture, and its challenges. Many Boards underestimate this.\n\nOne of the major external shocks we faced was the withdrawal of the GSP+ concession. For exporters like us, this was a serious blow. GSP+ allowed reduced tariffs when exporting to the European Union. Without it, our products became less competitive overnight.\n\nI took the trouble to understand the issue deeply, learning from experts in trade policy. It was clear that the concession had been granted on certain conditions, which the Government had failed to meet. Instead of engaging constructively with the European Union, political rhetoric took over.\n\nAt a meeting with the EU Ambassador, he made a striking remark: \u201cAn international cricket match must be played on a proper pitch\u2014not on the road.\u201d In other words, he maintained that this issue should be negotiated by the government with the European Union rather than making disparaging remarks about the scheme or making demands. The message was clear. International trade requires discipline and adherence to agreed-upon rules."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-10-islandlk-02433925",
    "num": "islandlk-02433925",
    "date_str": "2026-05-10",
    "description": "Leadership Under Fire \u2013 Lessons from Dankotuwa \u2013 Episode 4",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/leadership-under-fire-lessons-from-dankotuwa-episode-4/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1778352120.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-05-10-islandlk-02433925-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "dc2f4f51ad9c325eff0cc2fd30036159",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1717,
    "chunk_text": "At a meeting with the EU Ambassador, he made a striking remark: \u201cAn international cricket match must be played on a proper pitch\u2014not on the road.\u201d In other words, he maintained that this issue should be negotiated by the government with the European Union rather than making disparaging remarks about the scheme or making demands. The message was clear. International trade requires discipline and adherence to agreed-upon rules.\n\nYet, despite these setbacks, we managed to retain most of our customers. Relationships, built over years, proved more resilient than policy shocks.\n\nThen came the 2008\u20132009 global financial crisis. I happened to be in the United States at the time and saw first-hand the panic following the collapse of Lehman Brothers.\n\nThe impact on our business was immediate and severe. Our order book, which typically covered nine months, shrank to barely half a month. Major customers cancelled orders. European buyers struggled to make payments as their banks tightened credit.\n\nThe nature of our product made matters worse. Porcelain is not an essential purchase. Unlike garments or vehicles, which must be replaced regularly, porcelain items last for generations. In times of crisis, they are the last thing customers buy.\n\nThe Government\u2019s response did not help. An incentive scheme was introduced to reward exporters who increased exports. It was a well-intentioned but impractical idea. Demand had collapsed. No amount of persuasion could make buyers order what they could not sell.\n\nInternally, the pressure mounted. Salaries were delayed. Payments to suppliers were stretched. Fortunately, our banks and suppliers\u2014both local and foreign\u2014showed understanding and gave us time."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-10-islandlk-02433925",
    "num": "islandlk-02433925",
    "date_str": "2026-05-10",
    "description": "Leadership Under Fire \u2013 Lessons from Dankotuwa \u2013 Episode 4",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/leadership-under-fire-lessons-from-dankotuwa-episode-4/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1778352120.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-05-10-islandlk-02433925-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "85dd1a49ea312c23285d7dc161d756c6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1954,
    "chunk_text": "The Government\u2019s response did not help. An incentive scheme was introduced to reward exporters who increased exports. It was a well-intentioned but impractical idea. Demand had collapsed. No amount of persuasion could make buyers order what they could not sell.\n\nInternally, the pressure mounted. Salaries were delayed. Payments to suppliers were stretched. Fortunately, our banks and suppliers\u2014both local and foreign\u2014showed understanding and gave us time.\n\nIt was difficult to remain patient. But such moments reminded me that communication is often as important as strategy. People resist what they do not understand.\n\nWe undertook an educational project to inform our floor-level employees about the current global situation and what other Asian factories were experiencing. The message sank in for some, but not for others who believed that labour must never co-operate with management.\n\nIn the midst of this crisis, a foreign bank suddenly informed us that it was withdrawing all our facilities. Their regional office had classified the ceramic industry as declining and not sustainable.\n\nI was furious. I recalled a remark often made by the respected banker Edgar Gunatunga\u2014that foreign banks give you an umbrella when the weather is fine and take it away when it rains.\n\nI wrote a strong letter to the bank, quoting this very statement. The letter created quite a stir. Senior officials requested a meeting, and after detailed discussions, they restored our facilities. In a gesture of humour, they even presented me with an umbrella.\n\nMore importantly, I had already prepared contingency plans. Local bankers, including Mr Anil Amarasuriya, had assured support if needed. The key was to maintain credibility. We made it a point never to default on our obligations.\n\nTo reduce working capital, we introduced lean management practices and reduced raw material inventory. In theory, it was sound. In practice, it failed\u2014at least initially."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-10-islandlk-02433925",
    "num": "islandlk-02433925",
    "date_str": "2026-05-10",
    "description": "Leadership Under Fire \u2013 Lessons from Dankotuwa \u2013 Episode 4",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/leadership-under-fire-lessons-from-dankotuwa-episode-4/",
