    "chunk_id": "2026-05-10-islandlk-02433925-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "bcedad7235950b1b34c426a3826d0251",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1914,
    "chunk_text": "More importantly, I had already prepared contingency plans. Local bankers, including Mr Anil Amarasuriya, had assured support if needed. The key was to maintain credibility. We made it a point never to default on our obligations.\n\nTo reduce working capital, we introduced lean management practices and reduced raw material inventory. In theory, it was sound. In practice, it failed\u2014at least initially.\n\nPorcelain production is highly sensitive. Even slight changes in raw material composition affect shrinkage, colour, and strength. Our laboratory team had to constantly experiment to maintain quality.\n\nThis was a difficult period. However, we eventually stabilised the process and made lean management work in a more adapted form. It taught me that management techniques cannot be applied blindly. They must be adapted to the context.\n\nTwo important lessons stand out from this phase of my career.\n\nFirst, leadership is situational. The qualities required in a CEO vary depending on the organisation\u2019s stage and challenges. A technically competent or highly intelligent leader may still fail if they lack emotional intelligence or contextual understanding. This aligns with what modern management calls contingency theory\u2014there is no single best style of leadership.\n\nSecond, resilience is built through relationships and credibility. During the financial crisis, it was not policies or systems that saved us. It was the trust we had built with banks, suppliers, and customers. This reflects an important but often overlooked principle: organisations survive crises not merely through strategy, but through social capital.\n\nLooking back, many of these experiences reflect well-known management ideas\u2014but experienced in raw form.\n\nThe repeated failures in CEO selection highlight the limits of traditional \u201crational\u201d decision-making models and reinforce the importance of cultural and behavioural fit."
  },
