    "chunk_text": "Looking back, many of these experiences reflect well-known management ideas\u2014but experienced in raw form.\n\nThe repeated failures in CEO selection highlight the limits of traditional \u201crational\u201d decision-making models and reinforce the importance of cultural and behavioural fit.\n\nThe lean management failure illustrates the danger of applying best practices without considering environmental uncertainty\u2014what modern theory would describe as a misalignment between system design and supply chain risk.\n\nFinally, the banking episode is a classic example of reputation capital and relationship-based management, concepts that are now widely discussed but were then simply matters of instinct and experience.\n\nManagement, I have come to realise, is rarely neat or predictable. It is messy, human, and often contradictory. What works in one situation may fail in another. What appears logical may not work in practice. And what seems like a setback may later prove to be a valuable lesson.\n\nDankotuwa tested all these dimensions. It was not an easy journey. But it was one that deepened my understanding of management far beyond what any textbook could offer.\n\nAnd perhaps that is the real lesson: theory explains, but experience teaches.\n\n(Sunil G. Wijesinha is a Consultant on Productivity and Japanese Management Techniques\n\nFormer Chairman / Director of several listed and unlisted companies\n\nRecipient of the APO Regional Award for Promoting Productivity in the Asia-Pacific Region\n\nRecipient of the Order of the Rising Sun, Gold and Silver Rays \u2013 Government of Japan\n\nServing as MR\u2019s Deputy Finance Minister and the travel the job entailed\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
