    "chunk_size_bytes": 1890,
    "chunk_text": "Tourists to Hikkaduwa drop by nearly 80 percent as West Asian war exacts staggering toll\n\nHotels, home stays and restaurants in the popular tourist resort of Hikkaduwa are facing severe financial hardships as tourist arrivals have dropped by nearly 80% due to the absence of a long-term solution to US-Iran tensions, high airfares and other global factors.\n\nDickson Dewasiri, a member of the Hikkaduwa Tourist Hotel Owners Association, told The Island Financial Review that on average more than 70% of tourists visiting Sri Lanka patronize Hikkaduwa. \u201cHowever, today we hardly see any tourists, and this is a very sad situation, he said.\n\n\u2018Hikkaduwa is one of the most popular tourist destinations in Sri Lanka and more than 15,000 hotels, home stays, restaurants and tourist-related service providers operate in the area. Due to tensions in the Middle East, businesses across most hotels and restaurants have been severely affected, leading to the shutdown of many operations, Dewasiri explained.\n\nDewasiri added: \u2018As a result, more than 100,000 tourism-related direct and indirect jobs are now at stake in the area.\u2019\n\nOne of the Association\u2019s active members, meanwhile said:\u2018Hotel and restaurant owners now find it extremely difficult to maintain their properties. Therefore, some entities have reduced staff and even introduced service breaks for employees.\n\nHe added that many staff members are highly trained and skilled, but some are now leaving the profession and entering other businesses to survive.\n\n\u2018The exodus of skilled employees from the tourism sector will have a long-term impact due to the shortage of experienced personnel in the industry.\u2019\n\nDewasiri, who also owns and operates Devasiri Seafood and two hotels with around 25 rooms in the area said that most hotel and restaurant owners are in dire straits as many property owners have obtained bank loans."
